      "nets": ["RST", "GND", "SDA", "PWM", "+5V"],
      "observed": "The design contains both an ATtiny45-20P (U2) and an ATtiny13V-10S (U3), and their corresponding pins are wired to the same nets, including reset, ground, supply, and I/O. In particular, both MCU PB1 pins drive the shared PWM net.",
      "expected": "A fan controller should have one active MCU, or clearly indicate that these are mutually exclusive assembly alternatives. The files show both components without a stated population option; if both are fitted, their outputs can contend and their programs may behave incompatibly.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "PWM : J4.1(MISO,passive) R2.1(passive) R5.2(passive) U2.6(PB1,bidirectional) U3.6(PB1,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "RST : J1.4(Pin_4,passive) J4.5(~{RST},passive) R3.2(passive) U2.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional) U3.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "+5V : ... U2.8(VCC,power_in) U3.8(VCC,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "Atmel/ATtiny-centric boards: ... an ATtiny-driven fan controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If both MCUs are populated, they operate in parallel on shared signals. Conflicting PWM outputs could cause unreliable fan control or damage; differing firmware or reset behavior could also prevent intended operation. If they are intended as alternatives, the design files do not document that assembly constraint.",
      "confidence": 0.78
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist connects two separate ATtiny devices pin-for-pin to the same controller nets, including the PWM output. This is unsafe if both are assembled; the files do not identify them as mutually exclusive alternatives."
}

